{"term":"submerge","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:38bd00a53070a4b7c5","definition":"To sink out of sight.","example":"The submarine submerged in the water.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["submerse"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"intransitive","wordFamily":["resubmerge","submergeable","submergement","submergence","submerger","submergible"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/submerge#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:cdf15cf17398d4bb39","definition":"To put into a liquid; to immerse; to plunge into and keep in.","example":"In films, many people are murdered by being submerged in swimming pools.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["immerse"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":["resubmerge","submergeable","submergement","submergence","submerger","submergible"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/submerge#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:ca94945bfad28216de","definition":"To be below the surface of the sea, a lake, river, etc.","example":"On the day of RAIL 's site visit, in heavy weather, the scaffolding and decking that engineers stand on were submerged deep under choppy water, with work suspended. \"We have to work around the tides,\" explained Project Director Alan Venables. \"The wind pushes the tide up and the waves get larger. That causes some problems with the scaffold.\"","pos":"verb","synonyms":["submerse"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":["resubmerge","submergeable","submergement","submergence","submerger","submergible"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/submerge#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:cb95fa900e15224a10","definition":"To engulf or overwhelm.","example":"Because of the death of his father, he is submerged in sorrow.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["submerse"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"figuratively","register":"figuratively, transitive","wordFamily":["resubmerge","submergeable","submergement","submergence","submerger","submergible"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/submerge#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:24f2ca7f7e51d095f7","definition":"To drown or suppress.","example":"Mrs Dibble made for the kitchen, stumping violently with her crutches and heaving her bulk along with the obvious determination to submerge her wrongs by resorting to the gin-bottle.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["submerse"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"figuratively","register":"figuratively, transitive","wordFamily":["resubmerge","submergeable","submergement","submergence","submerger","submergible"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/submerge#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"}],"pronunciation":{"ipa":"/səbˈmɜːd͡ʒ/"},"wordFamily":["resubmerge","submergeable","submergement","submergence","submerger","submergible"]}